# The distinction that catches people: `gitleaks git` reads git history, so it
# finds a key you committed and then deleted. `gitleaks dir` reads the files on
# disk and knows nothing about history. A clean `dir` run says nothing about
# what is still in your commits.
name:    gitleaks
what:    find secrets in a repo — in the working tree, or anywhere in history
check:   gitleaks
install: sudo dnf install gitleaks   /   see github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks
docs:    gitleaks <command> --help
probe:   help

= THE TWO SCANS
@gitleaks git    | scan commit history — what you committed and then removed
  --no-banner    | no ASCII art, for a script or a hook
  --log-opts="--all" | every ref, not just the current branch
gitleaks dir .   | scan the files on disk, history ignored
gitleaks dir FILE | one file

= BEFORE A REPO GOES PUBLIC
gitleaks git --redact | show the findings with the secret itself masked
gitleaks git -v  | verbose: the matching line and where it came from
gitleaks git --exit-code 1 | non-zero when anything is found. For CI

= A REPORT YOU CAN KEEP
@gitleaks git    | with a report written out
  -f json -r out.json | machine-readable
  -f sarif -r out.sarif | what code-scanning UIs read
  -f csv -r out.csv | a spreadsheet

= WHEN IT FINDS SOMETHING THAT IS FINE
gitleaks:allow   | a comment on the line; gitleaks skips it
.gitleaksignore  | a file of fingerprints to ignore, one per line
-b baseline.json | treat everything in that report as already-known
-c .gitleaks.toml | your own rules, replacing the defaults

= FROM A PIPE
echo "$VALUE" \| gitleaks stdin | check one value without writing it anywhere

= WHEN IT DOES FIND A REAL KEY
                 | Rotate it first. Removing it from history does not un-leak
                 | something that has already been pushed or cloned
git filter-repo --replace-text FILE | rewrite history. Read its docs first:
                 | a bare `#` line in that file rewrites every `#` you have

= ALL FLAGS (from --help)
